const e=[{id:"pd-l-police-stop",moduleId:"m05-criminal-procedure",title:"When the police stop you",minutes:20,summary:"What turns a stop into an arrest, the three things you can ask for, and the clock that starts the moment you are no longer free to go.",plants:["pd-c-cp-01","pd-c-cp-02","pd-c-cp-03","pd-c-cp-04","pd-c-cp-05"],prepares:["pd-p-guided-arrest"],reading:[{bookId:"janab-criminal-procedure",where:"the early chapters on arrest and on the rights of a person arrested"}],source:"Federal Constitution Arts 5(3) and 5(4); Criminal Procedure Code (Act 593), including the First Schedule and s 28A.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Read Arts 5(3) and 5(4) in a current reprint of the Federal Constitution. The rights on arrest in the Criminal Procedure Code have been amended more than once, so read s 28A in a current reprint before relying on the detail here. The case law on when access to a lawyer must be allowed is live and contested — read it in full rather than in summary.",plate:"roadside",plateCaption:"Stopped, asked, arrested. Three different things, with different powers attached, and the difference matters at the roadside.",readMinutes:9,sections:[{id:"sec-pd-l-police-stop-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Most people will never read a contract in a courtroom; many will meet a police officer.",h:"Why start here",body:[{t:"p",text:"Most people will never read a contract in a court room. Many will meet a police officer. This lesson is about that meeting, in the order it happens."},{t:"p",text:"It is written for the person it happens to, and for the family at home waiting for news. You need no legal training to follow it."}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-police-stop-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Being stopped and questioned is not an arrest — an arrest is when you are no longer free to leave.",h:"Stopped is not arrested",body:[{t:"p",text:"Faizal drives a lorry in Ipoh. One night the police stop him at a roadblock. They ask for his identity card and look in the cab."},{t:"p",text:"Nothing has happened to Faizal in law yet. He has been stopped and questioned. When the officers finish, he drives away."},{t:"rule",text:"Being stopped and questioned is not an arrest. An arrest is the point at which a person is taken into custody and is no longer free to leave.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code (Act 593); standard practice"},{t:"caution",text:"The line is drawn by control, not by words. Nobody has to say the word arrest. If you are cuffed, held, or put in a vehicle, you are under arrest whatever it is called."},{t:"diagram",kind:"branch",title:"The one question that tells you which it is",caption:"Nobody has to say the word arrest. The line is drawn by control. If you are cuffed, held, or put in a vehicle, you are under arrest whatever anyone calls it.",alt:"A question — are you free to walk away? — branching two ways. Yes: you have been stopped and questioned, and in law nothing has happened to you yet. No, because you are cuffed, held or put in a vehicle: you are under arrest, and the protections that follow an arrest apply.",question:"Are you free to walk away?",branches:[{cond:"yes",label:"Stopped and questioned",note:"in law, nothing has happened yet"},{cond:"no",label:"Under arrest",note:"whatever it is called"}]}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-police-stop-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"For a seizable offence police may arrest without a warrant; otherwise one is generally needed.",h:"What an arrest is",body:[{t:"p",text:"An arrest takes away your liberty. The officer takes you into custody, either by touching you or because you submit and go along."},{t:"rule",text:"For a seizable offence a police officer may arrest without a warrant. For other offences a warrant or an order is generally needed.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code (Act 593), First Schedule"},{t:"p",text:"A seizable offence is simply one the Code marks for arrest without a warrant. Which offences those are is looked up in the First Schedule, not worked out by argument."},{t:"example",text:"Two neighbours fight outside a kedai runcit. One is arrested at the scene. The other is told to come to the station on Friday. The difference is how the Code classifies each offence, not who shouted louder."}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-police-stop-3",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Ask for three things: the grounds, a lawyer, and to tell your family.",h:"Three things to ask for",body:[{t:"p",text:"The Constitution gives an arrested person rights that start at once. They are short, and they are worth knowing by heart."},{t:"rule",text:"Article 5(3): a person arrested shall be informed as soon as may be of the grounds of his arrest. He shall also be allowed to consult and be defended by a lawyer of his choice.",source:"Federal Constitution, Art 5(3)"},{t:"p",text:"The Criminal Procedure Code adds a right people forget. Somebody at home may be told where you are."},{t:"rule",text:"The Code requires the police to let an arrested person contact a relative or friend, and a lawyer of his choice.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code (Act 593), s 28A"},{t:"list",items:["Ask what offence you are being held for. You are entitled to be told.","Ask for someone at home to be told where you are.","Ask for a lawyer, by name if you have one.","Ask for the officer's name and the station you are being taken to."]},{t:"caution",text:"When the right to a lawyer must be honoured is fought over in the courts. Police often say later. Ask anyway, ask again, and have someone write down each time you asked."}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-police-stop-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Twenty-four hours, then a Magistrate. That clock is in the Constitution, not just the Code.",h:"The twenty-four hour clock",body:[{t:"p",text:"The most useful number in this module is twenty-four. It is the limit on police detention without a Magistrate."},{t:"rule",text:"Article 5(4): a person arrested and not released shall be produced before a Magistrate within twenty-four hours, and shall not be detained further without the Magistrate's authority.",source:"Federal Constitution, Art 5(4)"},{t:"example",text:"Faizal is arrested at nine on Sunday night. By nine on Monday night he must be released or taken before a Magistrate. If neither has happened, his family should be asking why."},{t:"caution",text:"The twenty-four hours leaves out the time needed to travel to the Magistrate. In a rural district that can matter. It is not a licence for a second night in the lock-up."}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-police-stop-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"You must answer truthfully, but not a question whose answer would expose you to a charge.",h:"Answering questions",body:[{t:"p",text:"People assume they must answer everything, or that they may answer nothing. Neither is the Malaysian position."},{t:"rule",text:"A person questioned by the police must answer truthfully. He need not answer a question whose answer would expose him to a criminal charge or a penalty.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code (Act 593), provisions on police statements"},{t:"p",text:"So your name and address are not optional. Explaining where the phone in your bag came from is a different matter."},{t:"caution",text:"A statement given to the police can be used later. Never sign one you have not read. If you cannot read it, say so and ask for it to be read out to you."}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-police-stop-6",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Write down the times afterwards — memory of a bad night is what the later argument turns on.",h:"Write it down afterwards",body:[{t:"p",text:"Whatever happens, someone should keep a note. Memory of a bad night is unreliable, and the times are what the arguments later turn on."},{t:"list",items:["The time and place of the stop, and the time of the arrest.","What you were told, and what you asked for.","The names or numbers of the officers, and the station.","Every time a lawyer or a phone call was requested, and the answer given."]},{t:"p",text:"If the family cannot afford a lawyer, ask about the legal aid centre run by the Bar in the State. There is also a government legal aid scheme."}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-police-stop-7",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Nobody memorises their rights at eleven at night, which is why these five cards go in first.",h:"What to do with this",body:[{t:"p",text:"Five cards. They are the ones to have by heart before you need them, because nobody memorises rights at eleven at night in a police station."},{t:"p",text:"Then work the guided problem, which walks you through one arrest step by step. After that, the lesson From arrest to charge takes the classifications properly."}]}],quiz:[{id:"pd-q-ps1",q:"When does a police stop become an arrest?",options:["When the officer says the word arrest","When you are taken into custody and are no longer free to leave","When handcuffs are used","When you reach the police station"],answer:1,why:"It turns on control, not on any form of words. Handcuffs are not needed either.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code (Act 593); arrest turns on custody and control"},{id:"pd-q-ps2",q:"What must you be told when you are arrested?",options:["The name of the investigating officer","The grounds of your arrest, as soon as may be","The maximum sentence you face","Nothing at all until you are charged"],answer:1,why:"Article 5(3) says so. The trap is thinking the right only starts at the charge.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 5(3)"},{id:"pd-q-ps3",q:"After an arrest, who may you ask to contact?",options:["A lawyer of your choice, and a relative or friend","A lawyer only","A relative only","Nobody until questioning has finished"],answer:0,why:"Article 5(3) gives you the lawyer; the Code adds the relative or friend. Police often say later, so ask again and note the time.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 5(3); Criminal Procedure Code s 28A"},{id:"pd-q-ps4",q:"How long may the police hold you before a Magistrate must see you?",options:["Forty-eight hours","Seven days","Twenty-four hours","Until the investigation is finished"],answer:2,why:"Article 5(4). Longer needs a Magistrate's order, and travel time to the Magistrate is not counted.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 5(4)"},{id:"pd-q-ps5",q:"Must you answer a police question if the answer would expose you to a charge?",options:["Yes, always","No, though you must answer other questions truthfully","No, and you need not answer anything at all","Only if a lawyer is present"],answer:1,why:"The Code asks for truthful answers but protects you from ones that would incriminate you. Refusing to say anything at all is not the rule.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code (Act 593); the protection against self-incriminating answers"},{id:"pd-q-ps6",q:"Arrested at nine on Sunday night, still in the lock-up at noon on Tuesday, no Magistrate seen. What is the position?",options:["The detention has run past the constitutional limit","It is lawful because the investigation is not finished","It is lawful if a senior officer approved it","It is lawful for the first three days"],answer:0,why:"Twenty-four hours is the limit without a Magistrate, and only a remand order extends it.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 5(4)"}]},{id:"pd-l-first-court-day",moduleId:"m05-criminal-procedure",title:"The lock-up, the charge and the first day in court",minutes:20,summary:"Remand, the charge read out, the plea, and what bail and a surety really are — written for the family in the public gallery.",plants:["pd-c-cp-06","pd-c-cp-07","pd-c-cp-08","pd-c-cp-09","pd-c-cp-10"],prepares:["pd-p-remand-bail"],reading:[{bookId:"janab-criminal-procedure",where:"the chapters on remand, on charges, and on bail"}],source:"Criminal Procedure Code (Act 593), including s 117, s 388 and the First Schedule; Federal Constitution Art 5(4).",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"The maximum remand periods under s 117 of the Criminal Procedure Code are graduated by the sentence the offence carries and have been amended — check the current text. Bail classification comes from the First Schedule, and a special Act may displace it, so check the Act the charge is brought under. Bail figures and court practice differ between courts; nothing here is a substitute for asking in the court itself.",plate:"dock",plateCaption:"The first appearance: a charge read out, and a decision about liberty taken long before any trial.",readMinutes:7,sections:[{id:"sec-pd-l-first-court-day-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A night in the lock-up ends one of three ways, and knowing which tells you what to do next.",h:"Three roads out of the lock-up",body:[{t:"p",text:"A night in the lock-up ends in one of three ways. Knowing which one you are in tells you what to do next."},{t:"list",items:["Released with no charge. Sometimes with a warning, sometimes with nothing said at all.","Released on police bail, with a date to come back to the station.","Taken before a Magistrate, who is asked to allow the police more time."]},{t:"p",text:"The third road is the remand hearing. It is where families most often turn up with no idea what is about to happen."},{t:"diagram",kind:"branch",title:"Which road you are on",caption:"Knowing which of the three you are in tells you what to do next. Families most often arrive with no idea that the third one is about to happen.",alt:"A question — how does the night end? — branching three ways. Released with no charge, sometimes with a warning. Released on police bail, with a date to come back to the station. Or taken before a Magistrate, who is asked to allow the police more time.",question:"How does the night end?",branches:[{cond:"no charge",label:"Released",note:"a warning, or nothing said at all"},{cond:"police bail",label:"Released, with a date",note:"come back to the station"},{cond:"police want more time",label:"Before a Magistrate",note:"this is the remand hearing"}]}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-first-court-day-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Beyond twenty-four hours needs a Magistrate's remand order, made for the investigation.",h:"The remand hearing",body:[{t:"p",text:"Remand means holding a person while the police investigate. After the first twenty-four hours only a Magistrate can allow it."},{t:"rule",text:"Detention beyond twenty-four hours needs a remand order from a Magistrate. It is made for the investigation, and for the period the Magistrate thinks fit.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code (Act 593), s 117"},{t:"p",text:"The hearing is short and is often held in a small room. The investigating officer says why more time is needed. The person arrested may speak, and so may a lawyer."},{t:"example",text:"Danial is nineteen and has never been arrested. The police ask for seven days. His lawyer points out that the phone has already been seized, so four days would do. The Magistrate gives four."},{t:"caution",text:"Nobody is deciding guilt at a remand hearing. Arguing that Danial did not do it is the wrong argument. The question is whether more detention is really needed."}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-first-court-day-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A charge must give enough detail of time, place and person to be answered.",h:"Being charged",body:[{t:"p",text:"A charge is the written accusation read out in court. It is the first document in the case that truly belongs to the accused."},{t:"rule",text:"A charge must state the offence, with enough detail of time, place and person for the accused to know what he is answering.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code (Act 593), provisions on the form of charges"},{t:"example",text:"Theft of a mobile phone belonging to Puan Lim, at a shop on Jalan Melaka, on 3 March. That is enough. Theft of something, somewhere in Melaka, some time last year is not."},{t:"p",text:"The charge is read in a language the accused understands. If it is not understood, say so before pleading, not afterwards."}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-first-court-day-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Two real answers to a charge, and pleading guilty to get it over with is a decision with consequences.",h:"The plea",body:[{t:"p",text:"Once the charge is read the court asks for a plea. There are two real answers."},{t:"list",items:["Guilty. The court may convict at once, and the next step is sentence.","Not guilty, usually said in court as claiming trial. The prosecution must then prove the charge."]},{t:"p",text:"A guilty plea is not simply accepted. The court checks that the accused understands the charge and admits the facts behind it."},{t:"caution",text:"People plead guilty to get out of the lock-up sooner. It is the most expensive short cut in this module. A conviction stays on the record, and the sentence can be heavier than the wait."}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-first-court-day-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Bailable means entitled; non-bailable means the court decides.",h:"Bail, in plain words",body:[{t:"predict",prompt:"Danial's mother is told at the counter that he can come home today if she pays three thousand ringgit. What is that money?",options:["A fine for what he is accused of","A payment to the police for letting him out","A deposit that comes back if he attends every date"],answer:2,reveal:"The third, and the word for it is bail. It secures one thing: that Danial turns up. If you thought fine, you are in very good company."},{t:"p",text:"Bail is not a fine and it is not a punishment. It is a promise to come back to court, backed by money."},{t:"rule",text:"For a bailable offence the accused is entitled to be released on bail. For a non-bailable offence bail is in the discretion of the court.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code (Act 593), First Schedule and s 388"},{t:"p",text:"Which one applies is looked up rather than argued. The First Schedule to the Code classifies each offence."},{t:"p",text:"A surety, or penjamin, is a person who promises the court that the accused will attend. If the accused disappears, the surety can lose the money."},{t:"example",text:"Bail is set at three thousand ringgit with one surety. Danial's mother signs the bond and deposits the money. If he attends every date, it comes back at the end."},{t:"caution",text:"Some Acts carry their own bail rules, and a few shut bail out. Check the Act the charge is brought under, not only the Code."}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-first-court-day-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A criminal trial runs in the same order in a small case and a large one.",h:"How a trial runs",body:[{t:"p",text:"A criminal trial has an order, and it is the same order in a small case and a large one."},{t:"list",items:["The prosecution calls its witnesses. Each one can be cross-examined by the defence.","The court then decides whether there is a case to answer. If there is not, the accused is acquitted.","If there is, the defence is called. The accused may give evidence or stay silent.","The court gives its decision. If it convicts, it hears mitigation before passing sentence."]},{t:"p",text:"Mitigation is the accused's chance to explain his circumstances before sentence. It is not a denial of guilt."},{t:"caution",text:"Dates get adjourned, often. Several mention dates before a trial is normal and is not a sign that something has gone wrong."},{t:"p",text:"A mention date is a short hearing to move the case along. Nobody gives evidence at one. It is the day the court fixes the next thing to happen."},{t:"p",text:"If the family cannot pay for a lawyer, ask about legal aid before the next date, not on the morning of it."}]},{id:"sec-pd-l-first-court-day-6",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"The problem puts you in the mother's position, not the lawyer's.",h:"What to do with this",body:[{t:"p",text:"Five cards, then the problem question on remand and bail. It puts you in the position of the mother, not the lawyer."},{t:"p",text:"After that, the lesson on bail and the shape of a criminal trial takes the same ground at a harder level."}]}],quiz:[{id:"pd-q-fc1",q:"Who can allow the police to hold a person beyond twenty-four hours?",options:["The investigating officer","The Public Prosecutor","The officer in charge of the station","A Magistrate, by a remand order"],answer:3,why:"Remand is a judicial decision under s 117. The police ask; the Magistrate decides.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code s 117"},{id:"pd-q-fc2",q:"What is the question at a remand hearing?",options:["Whether further detention is needed for the investigation","Whether the accused is guilty","Whether bail should be granted","Whether the charge is properly drafted"],answer:0,why:"Guilt is not in issue yet. Arguing innocence there wastes the one argument you have.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code s 117"},{id:"pd-q-fc3",q:"What must a charge tell the accused?",options:["The offence, with enough detail of time, place and person","The likely sentence","The evidence the prosecution holds","The names of the witnesses"],answer:0,why:"A person cannot answer a case he has not been told. A vague charge is one to raise at once.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code (Act 593); the provisions on the form of a charge"},{id:"pd-q-fc4",q:"What does claiming trial mean?",options:["Asking for a faster hearing","Asking to be tried in a higher court","Admitting the facts but disputing the sentence","Pleading not guilty, so the prosecution must prove the charge"],answer:3,why:"It is the ordinary way a not guilty plea is described in a Malaysian court.",source:"Standard Malaysian court practice; claiming trial is a plea of not guilty"},{id:"pd-q-fc5",q:"What is a surety promising?",options:["That the accused is innocent","To pay any fine the court imposes","That the accused will attend court, with money at risk if he does not","To keep the accused at home"],answer:2,why:"Bail secures attendance. The surety's money is the security, and it comes back if the dates are kept.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code (Act 593); the bail and bond provisions"},{id:"pd-q-fc6",q:"When is bail a matter of right rather than of discretion?",options:["When the offence is classified as bailable","When the accused has no record","When the accused is under twenty-one","When the police do not object"],answer:0,why:"The First Schedule does the classifying. Non-bailable means the court has a discretion, not that bail is impossible.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code, First Schedule; ss 387 and 388"}]},{id:"l-criminal-procedure",moduleId:"m05-criminal-procedure",title:"From arrest to charge",minutes:30,summary:"How the Criminal Procedure Code sorts offences into classes, and what those classes decide about arrest, remand and bail.",plants:[],prepares:[],reading:[{bookId:"janab-criminal-procedure",where:"the chapters on arrest, remand, bail and the trial process"}],source:"Criminal Procedure Code (Act 593); Federal Constitution Arts 5(3), 5(4); Balachandran v PP [2005] 2 MLJ 301 (FC).",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Act 593 was amended by Act 846 in 2023 — read the current reprint rather than an older text, and do not quote section numbers for arrest, remand or bail from this page. Confirm the Balachandran citation and check whether the prima facie standard has been restated more recently.",plate:"charge-sheet",plateCaption:"A charge: what is alleged, in enough particulars of time, place and person to be answered.",readMinutes:9,glossaryDense:"Arrest, bail, seizable, bailable, non-bailable and remand are all introduced here, and each is a classification the reader must be able to look up mid-sentence.",sections:[{id:"sec-l-criminal-procedure-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Arrested on a Friday night: how long can they hold him, can he see a lawyer, and can he get out?",h:"The night of an arrest",body:[{t:"p",text:"Danial is arrested at eleven on a Friday night outside a mamak shop. His sister wants to know three things. How long can they hold him, can she get him a lawyer, and can he get out on bail?"},{t:"p",text:"Criminal procedure looks like a mountain of detail until you notice one thing. A small number of labels, set out in a schedule at the back of the Criminal Procedure Code, answer most of the questions people actually ask."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Danial's sister wants to know whether the police needed a warrant, and whether he can get bail. Before you read on — what decides those two answers?",options:["How serious the offence looks, and whether he seems likely to run","Which class the offence falls into in a schedule at the back of the Code","What the magistrate thinks once Danial is brought to court"],answer:1,reveal:"A schedule, in both cases. The other two answers describe something real, and neither of them is where you start. Seriousness and the risk of flight are what a court weighs once it has a discretion at all, and whether it has one is settled by the classification. So look the offence up first. Nearly every question the sister asked is answered by that single step."}]},{id:"sec-l-criminal-procedure-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Look the offence up in the First Schedule before reasoning about any of it.",h:"Look the offence up",body:[{t:"p",text:"Three questions come up again and again. May the police arrest without a warrant, may they investigate without an order, and is bail available? All three are settled by looking the offence up. They are not worked out by reasoning about them."},{t:"p",text:"A **warrant** is a written order from a court authorising an arrest. The Code sorts every offence into classes that decide when one is needed."},{t:"rule",text:"The First Schedule to the CPC classifies each offence as seizable or non-seizable, and as bailable, non-bailable or unbailable. Look the offence up before reasoning about the procedure.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code (Act 593), First Schedule"},{t:"diagram",kind:"branch",title:"One lookup answers three questions",caption:"The classification comes first and the powers follow from it. Arguing about whether an arrest was reasonable, before checking which column the offence sits in, is the common way to get this wrong.",alt:"A question — how does the First Schedule classify this offence? — branching two ways. Seizable: the police may arrest without a warrant and investigate without an order. Non-seizable: a warrant or a magistrate's order is generally needed for both.",question:"How does the First Schedule classify the offence?",branches:[{cond:"seizable",label:"Arrest without a warrant",note:"and investigate without an order"},{cond:"non-seizable",label:"Warrant, or an order",note:"generally needed for both"}]},{t:"caution",text:"Malaysia says *seizable* where England says *arrestable*. Borrowing the English word signals that you are working from an English book, and the two classifications are not identical. So it is more than a difference of naming."}]},{id:"sec-l-criminal-procedure-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Article 5(3) and 5(4) are the floor: grounds, a lawyer, and a magistrate within twenty-four hours.",h:"Arrest, and the floor beneath it",body:[{t:"p",text:"For a seizable offence a police officer may arrest without a warrant. For a non-seizable offence the general position is that a warrant or an order is required first."},{t:"p",text:"But those powers sit underneath constitutional guarantees that apply whatever the classification, and that is usually where the real argument is."},{t:"rule",text:"Article 5(3): a person arrested shall be informed as soon as may be of the grounds of his arrest and shall be allowed to consult and be defended by a legal practitioner of his choice.",source:"Federal Constitution, Art 5(3)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: they must tell you why you are being held, and let you see a lawyer of your own choosing."},{t:"rule",text:"Article 5(4): a person arrested and not released shall without unreasonable delay, and in any case within twenty-four hours, be produced before a magistrate and shall not be further detained without the magistrate's authority.",source:"Federal Constitution, Art 5(4)"},{t:"p",text:"The twenty-four hours does not count the time needed to travel to the magistrate. Holding somebody longer needs a **remand order**, which is a magistrate's permission to keep a person in custody while the investigation continues."},{t:"p",text:"The magistrate is deciding whether continued detention is genuinely justified for that investigation. The hearing is not meant to be a formality."},{t:"diagram",kind:"flow",title:"The clock from arrest to a magistrate",caption:"Twenty-four hours is a ceiling on police custody, not on detention. Past it the decision belongs to a magistrate, who is meant to ask whether the investigation genuinely needs the time.",alt:"Four steps. Arrest. Then, as soon as may be, the grounds of arrest and access to a lawyer of your own choosing under Article 5(3). Then production before a magistrate within twenty-four hours under Article 5(4), not counting travel time. Then either a remand order extending detention, or release.",steps:[{label:"Arrest",note:"no warrant needed if seizable"},{label:"Grounds, and a lawyer",note:"Art 5(3), as soon as may be"},{label:"Before a magistrate",note:"Art 5(4), within 24 hours"},{label:"Remand, or release",note:"the magistrate decides, not the police"}]},{t:"caution",text:"The right to a lawyer under Article 5(3) is not always given effect at the moment of arrest. The case law on when access must be allowed is worth reading in full rather than in summary. This is a live and contested area."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Danial was arrested at eleven on Friday night. It is Sunday morning and he has not been before a magistrate. What has gone wrong?",options:["Nothing — the police may hold him while they investigate","Article 5(4) required him to be produced before a magistrate within twenty-four hours","Nothing, so long as he has been allowed to see a lawyer"],answer:1,why:"Twenty-four hours is the outer limit, and only the travelling time to the magistrate is left out of the count. Holding him longer needs a remand order, and only a magistrate can give one. The first answer treats an unfinished investigation as its own authority to detain, which is the thing Article 5(4) refuses. The third mixes up two separate guarantees. A lawyer under 5(3) buys the police no extra hours."}]},{id:"sec-l-criminal-procedure-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Bail is release before trial, and whether it is a right or a discretion depends on the classification.",h:"Bail",body:[{t:"p",text:"**Bail** is release from custody before trial. The accused usually promises to come back to court, and money or a surety stands as security for that promise."},{t:"p",text:"Bail in Malaysia turns mostly on the classification of the offence rather than on a general discretion in the judge. That is a real difference from the English approach a common law textbook will describe."},{t:"list",items:["Bailable — bail is available as of right.","Non-bailable — bail is at the court's discretion.","Unbailable — bail is not available at all."]},{t:"p",text:"So the first question is never whether bail ought to be granted. It is which of those three boxes the offence sits in, and the Schedule answers that."},{t:"caution",text:"Special legislation frequently displaces the ordinary rules, and security and drugs legislation in particular carry their own bail provisions. Check whether the charge is under the Penal Code or under a particular Act before applying the general position."}]},{id:"sec-l-criminal-procedure-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A charge must give enough particulars of time, place and person to be answered.",h:"Investigation and the charge",body:[{t:"p",text:"A seizable offence may be investigated without an order, while a non-seizable one generally needs one. The investigation produces the investigation papers, which go to the Deputy Public Prosecutor, and the decision to prosecute belongs to the Public Prosecutor."},{t:"rule",text:"The charge must state the offence, and enough particulars of time, place, and the person or thing concerned, to give the accused notice of what he is charged with.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code (Act 593)"},{t:"p",text:"A defective charge is not automatically fatal, and the usual question is whether the accused was prejudiced or misled by it. But the drafting rules exist for a plain reason. Nobody can answer a case they have not been told."}]},{id:"sec-l-criminal-procedure-5",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"At the close of the prosecution case the court tests the evidence properly, not just whether some exists.",h:"The halfway point of a trial",body:[{t:"p",text:"The prosecution calls its witnesses and then closes its case. The court asks at that point whether a **prima facie case** has been made out, which means whether there is a case for the accused to answer."},{t:"p",text:"If there is, the accused is called on to enter his defence. If there is not, he is acquitted there and then."},{t:"rule",text:"A prima facie case requires the court to undertake a maximum evaluation of the prosecution evidence — to test its credibility — and to ask whether, if unrebutted, it would warrant a conviction.",source:"Balachandran v PP [2005] 2 MLJ 301 (FC)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: the judge must weigh how believable the prosecution's evidence really is, and ask whether it would be enough to convict if nobody answered it."},{t:"p",text:"The word *maximum* is doing real work there. A minimum evaluation would ask only whether there is some evidence on each element. That would put an accused to his defence on evidence no reasonable court could convict on."},{t:"p",text:"If the defence is called, the accused may give evidence on oath, make an unsworn statement from the dock, or say nothing at all. Each of those has different consequences. Conviction still requires proof beyond reasonable doubt on the whole of the evidence."}]},{id:"sec-l-criminal-procedure-6",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Read the First Schedule itself rather than a summary of it.",h:"What to do with this",body:[{t:"p",text:"There are no flashcards or problems for this module yet. The most useful thing you can do is read the First Schedule itself, rather than a summary of it."},{t:"p",text:"Follow one offence all the way through: its classification, the arrest power that follows from it, and the bail position that follows from that. The structure becomes obvious once you have done it once."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qcp1",q:"Where do you look to find whether an offence is seizable and bailable?",options:["The Penal Code section that creates it","The First Schedule to the Criminal Procedure Code","The charge sheet","The Courts of Judicature Act 1964"],answer:1,why:"Classification decides almost everything about the procedure, so look it up before you reason about it.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code, First Schedule"},{id:"qcp2",q:"Malaysia says 'seizable' where England says what?",options:["Indictable","Summary","Triable either way","Arrestable"],answer:3,why:"And the two classifications are not identical, so it is more than a difference of naming.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code, First Schedule; standard classification"},{id:"qcp3",q:"What does Article 5(3) require on arrest?",options:["That he be produced before a magistrate within twenty-four hours","That the charge be put in writing","That a lawyer be present at all times","That the person be told the grounds, and be allowed a lawyer of his choice"],answer:3,why:"Article 5(4) is the twenty-four hour provision. Keep the two apart.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 5(3)"},{id:"qcp4",q:"Which class means bail is at the court's discretion?",options:["Bailable","Non-bailable","Unbailable","Seizable"],answer:1,why:"Bailable means bail as of right. Unbailable means no bail at all.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code s 388 and the First Schedule"},{id:"qcp5",q:"What does 'maximum evaluation' mean at the close of the prosecution case?",options:["The court must take the evidence at its highest","The prosecution must call every possible witness","The accused must be called to give evidence","The court must test how believable the prosecution evidence is, not merely note that it exists"],answer:3,why:"The question is whether that evidence, left unanswered, would warrant a conviction.",source:"Balachandran v PP [2005] 2 MLJ 301 (FC)"}]},{id:"l-finding-facts",moduleId:"m05-criminal-procedure",title:"Who decides what happened",summary:"A trial answers two different kinds of question. Knowing which one you are arguing, and who decides it, changes how you argue.",minutes:26,readMinutes:8,reading:[{statuteId:"criminal-procedure-code",where:"the provisions governing the conduct of a trial and the recording of findings — read them for who decides what, and note the sections yourself"},{bookId:"janab-criminal-procedure",where:"on the structure of a trial and on appeals against findings of fact"}],source:"The distinction between questions of fact and law, and the standard on appeal against findings of fact, are general common law doctrine. No section of the Criminal Procedure Code is stated, and nothing is asserted about the history of lay participation in Malaysian trials, because neither has been verified here.",verify:"Check the Criminal Procedure Code provisions on trial and on appeal in the current published version. If you need the position on lay participation or its history, verify it from a source rather than from this lesson, which deliberately does not state it.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",sections:[{id:"sec-l-finding-facts-0",h:"Two questions in every trial",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"What happened is a question of fact; what follows legally is a question of law, and they are decided differently.",body:[{t:"p",text:"Every contested trial contains two kinds of question and they behave differently."},{t:"p",text:"Questions of fact: what happened, who did it, what was said."},{t:"p",text:"Questions of law: what the provision means, whether the established facts satisfy it."},{t:"p",text:"The distinction matters most on appeal, where an appellate court will interfere far more readily with the second than with the first."}]},{id:"sec-l-finding-facts-1",h:"Why findings of fact are hard to disturb",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"The trial court saw the witnesses, and an appellate court reading a transcript is not better placed to assess them.",body:[{t:"p",text:"The reason is practical rather than deferential."},{t:"p",text:"The trial court watched the witnesses give evidence and be cross-examined. An appellate court has a transcript."},{t:"p",text:"A transcript does not record hesitation, or the answer that arrived too quickly, so a court reading one is not better placed to decide who was telling the truth."},{t:"predict",prompt:"An appellant says the judge believed the wrong witness. How promising is that ground?",options:["Strong — appellate courts review the whole case afresh","Weak on its own — it asks the appellate court to redo an assessment it is worse placed to make"],answer:1,reveal:"It is not impossible, but it needs more: an inconsistency with undisputed documents, or a reason the assessment could not stand on the evidence. Simply preferring the other witness is the ground least likely to succeed, and knowing that redirects effort towards questions of law."}]},{id:"sec-l-finding-facts-2",h:"Who does the finding",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"In a Malaysian trial both questions are decided by the judge, which shapes how each is argued.",body:[{t:"p",text:"In some systems fact and law are split between different decision-makers, with lay fact-finders deciding what happened and a judge ruling on law."},{t:"p",text:"In a Malaysian trial the judge decides both."},{t:"p",text:"That has a consequence for advocacy. Where the same person rules on admissibility and then decides the facts, they have necessarily seen material they may have excluded."},{t:"p",text:"It also means reasons are given. A fact-finder who must explain a finding in writing is subject to a check that an unexplained verdict is not."},{t:"caution",text:"This lesson does not describe the history of lay participation in Malaysian trials or when it changed, because that has not been verified in this corpus. Check it before asserting anything about it."}]},{id:"sec-l-finding-facts-3",h:"Arguing to the right question",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Identify whether your point is one of fact or law before choosing how to make it.",body:[{t:"p",text:"The practical payoff is in how you spend your time."},{t:"p",text:"A point of fact is argued from the evidence — inconsistencies, documents, what is inherently likely."},{t:"p",text:"A point of law is argued from the provision and the authorities, and it is the one that travels on appeal."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"You have a weak factual case and an arguable point of construction. Where should the effort go?",options:["The facts, because they decide the case","The point of construction, which is also the ground most likely to survive an appeal"],answer:1,why:"Effort should follow where it can actually change an outcome. A construction point can be decided in your favour on the material as it stands, and it is reviewable if it goes the other way."}]}],quiz:[{id:"q-crimpro-ff-1",q:"Why are findings of fact rarely disturbed on appeal?",options:["Appeals are discouraged","Facts are not appealable at all","Only the prosecution may appeal facts","The trial court saw the witnesses and an appellate court reading a transcript is not better placed to assess them"],answer:3,why:"The reason is about who is better placed. A transcript loses everything about how evidence was given, which is much of what a credibility assessment rests on."},{id:"q-crimpro-ff-2",q:"What follows from the same judge deciding both admissibility and the facts?",options:["Nothing of significance","They will have seen material they may have ruled inadmissible, and they must give reasons for their findings","Admissibility is never argued","The trial must be split in two"],answer:1,why:"Both consequences are real and they pull in opposite directions. Exposure to excluded material is a risk. The duty to give written reasons is a safeguard, and an unexplained verdict carries no such thing."}]},{id:"l-bail-trial",moduleId:"m05-criminal-procedure",title:"Bail, and the shape of a criminal trial",minutes:30,summary:"Whether bail is available turns on how the offence is classified. The trial then stops twice to ask a question, and the two questions are not the same one.",plants:["ms-crimpro-01","ms-crimpro-02","ms-crimpro-03","ms-crimpro-04","ms-crimpro-05"],prepares:[],reading:[{bookId:"janab-criminal-procedure",where:"the chapters on bail, remand and the conduct of a summary trial"}],source:"Criminal Procedure Code ss 117, 180, 387, 388 and the First Schedule; Federal Constitution Arts 5(3), 5(4) and 145(3).",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Section 117 remand periods and the terms of s 388 have both been amended. Check the current text of each, and check the current authority on what 'prima facie case' requires under s 180 before relying on the description of maximum evaluation above.",plate:"bail",plateCaption:"The ledger and the key. The First Schedule, not the seriousness of the offence, decides which of them the accused is entitled to.",readMinutes:10,sections:[{id:"sec-l-bail-trial-0",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Bailable means as of right; non-bailable means discretionary, not unavailable.",anchor:"fig-l-bail-trial-0",h:"Three kinds of offence",body:[{t:"p",text:"Someone is arrested on a Friday night. The first thing the family asks is whether he can come home while the case runs. That is bail."},{t:"p",text:"Bail is a promise to come back to court, usually backed by money or by a surety. A surety is a person who agrees to be answerable if the accused does not turn up."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Someone is arrested on a Friday night. The charge is listed in the Code as a **non-bailable** offence. Can he come home before the trial?",options:["No — that is what the word means","Yes, if the court decides to allow it","Only once he has been charged in court and has pleaded"],answer:1,reveal:"He can, if the court is willing. Non-bailable does not mean bail is unavailable. It means bail is in the court's hands rather than the accused's right. The word misleads everybody the first time. Read it as 'bail is not automatic'."},{t:"p",text:"Whether bail is available depends on how the offence is classified, and the First Schedule to the Criminal Procedure Code does that offence by offence. The vocabulary misleads everybody the first time: 'non-bailable' does not mean bail is unavailable."},{t:"list",items:["**Bailable offence** — the accused is entitled to be released on bail. It is not a discretion.","**Non-bailable offence** — bail is in the discretion of the court, subject to the restrictions in s 388.","**Offences punishable with death or imprisonment for life** — the general position under s 388(1) is that a person shall not be released on bail. The section then has provisos, which include provision for young persons, women, and the sick or infirm."]},{t:"rule",text:"A person accused of a bailable offence shall be released on bail; a person accused of a non-bailable offence may be released on bail, in the discretion of the court.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code ss 387 and 388"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: bailable means the court must grant bail. Non-bailable means the court may."},{t:"caution",text:"Some statutes outside the Code create their own bail regimes, and some exclude bail altogether. Always check the statute creating the offence before applying the Code's scheme."},{t:"table",caption:"What the classification decides",columns:[{label:"Classification"},{label:"Bail"},{label:"Where it is found"}],rows:[["Bailable","As of right — the accused shall be released","First Schedule, CPC"],["Non-bailable","In the court's discretion under s 388 — *not* unavailable","First Schedule, CPC"],["Punishable with death or life imprisonment","Restricted by s 388(1), subject to its provisos","s 388 itself"]],note:"Special legislation, particularly on security and drugs, often displaces this scheme entirely. Check the statute creating the offence before applying the Code.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code ss 387, 388 and the First Schedule"}]},{id:"sec-l-bail-trial-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Where bail is discretionary the real question is whether he will come back, and what happens meanwhile.",h:"How the court uses its discretion",body:[{t:"p",text:"Where bail is discretionary, the court is really asking one thing. Will this person come back to court, and what happens meanwhile if he is out?"},{t:"p",text:"The matters weighed are practical ones: whether the accused will run, whether he may interfere with witnesses, how grave the offence is and what sentence it carries. The court also weighs his antecedents, his ties to the place, and the state of the investigation."},{t:"p",text:"Conditions are how those risks are managed — sureties, surrender of a passport, reporting to a police station. Proposing conditions is usually a stronger argument than an assertion that the risk is low."},{t:"example",text:"Encik Zul has run a stall in Ipoh for twenty years and his children are at school there. He is charged with a non-bailable offence. Two local sureties, his passport held by the court, and reporting every Monday answer most of what the court is worried about."}]},{id:"sec-l-bail-trial-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Grounds, a lawyer, and a Magistrate within twenty-four hours — the constitutional floor on arrest.",h:"Arrest, and the clock",body:[{t:"p",text:"The Constitution puts a clock on police custody, and it is a short one."},{t:"rule",text:"A person arrested shall be informed as soon as may be of the grounds of his arrest and shall be allowed to consult and be defended by a legal practitioner of his choice; and shall not be detained beyond twenty-four hours without the authority of a Magistrate.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 5(3) and 5(4), reflected in the Criminal Procedure Code"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: you must be told why you were arrested, you may see a lawyer, and after twenty-four hours only a Magistrate can authorise keeping you."},{t:"p",text:"To hold someone longer the police must go to a Magistrate for a remand order under section 117 of the Code. Remand is detention authorised for the purpose of investigation, for the period the Magistrate thinks fit."},{t:"p",text:"That hearing is a real hearing and not a formality. The police must show why further detention is needed."},{t:"caution",text:"Check the current maximum remand periods in section 117. They are graduated by the length of the sentence the offence carries, and they have been amended."}]},{id:"sec-l-bail-trial-3",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Prima facie case at the close of the prosecution; beyond reasonable doubt at the close of the defence.",anchor:"fig-l-bail-trial-1",h:"The two decision points at trial",body:[{t:"p",text:"A criminal trial stops twice and asks a question. The two questions are different, and merging them is the classic error."},{t:"p",text:"The first stop comes when the prosecution has finished calling its evidence."},{t:"rule",text:"At the close of the case for the prosecution the court shall consider whether the prosecution has made out a prima facie case; if it has, the court shall call upon the accused to enter his defence, and if it has not, the court shall record an order of acquittal.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code s 180"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: at the halfway point the court asks whether the prosecution's evidence, standing alone, would be enough to convict. If it would not, the case ends there and then."},{t:"p",text:"A prima facie case is made out where the evidence, if unrebutted, would warrant a conviction. Prima facie means 'on the face of it' — enough unless it is answered."},{t:"p",text:"That standard has been amended and much discussed. The phrase 'maximum evaluation' comes from that discussion. It means the court must properly evaluate the prosecution evidence at this stage, rather than merely ask whether some evidence exists."},{t:"p",text:"At the close of the defence the question is different and simpler. On the whole of the evidence, has the prosecution proved its case beyond reasonable doubt? A defence that raises a reasonable doubt is enough, and it does not have to be believed."},{t:"example",text:"Two witnesses say they saw Kumar near the shop. Kumar says he was elsewhere and calls his sister to say so. The court need not believe the sister. If her evidence leaves the court unsure, that is enough for Kumar."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"At the close of the defence, what does the accused have to do?",options:["Prove his account, on the balance of probabilities","Nothing — the prosecution must prove its case beyond reasonable doubt","Show a prima facie case of his own"],answer:1,why:"On the whole of the evidence, the question is whether the prosecution has proved its case. A defence that leaves the court unsure is enough, and it does not have to be believed. The first answer merges the two stages. That burden appears only where a statute puts it on him."},{t:"caution",text:"Do not merge the two stages. Writing that the accused must *prove* his defence at the second stage is wrong. The exception is where a statute places a burden on him, as s 105 of the Evidence Act does for the general exceptions."},{t:"compare",title:"The two decision points ask different questions",left:{h:"Close of the prosecution case — s 180",items:["Has a *prima facie* case been made out?","Evidence which, if unrebutted, would warrant a conviction.","The court evaluates the prosecution evidence properly rather than asking whether any evidence exists.","If not made out: acquittal."]},right:{h:"Close of the defence — s 182A",items:["Has the prosecution proved its case beyond reasonable doubt on the whole of the evidence?","The defence need only raise a reasonable doubt.","It does not have to be believed.","The burden has not shifted, save where a statute shifts it."]}}]},{id:"sec-l-bail-trial-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Article 145(3) puts prosecutions in the hands of the Public Prosecutor, not the complainant.",h:"Who decides to prosecute",body:[{t:"p",text:"The Public Prosecutor has control and direction of all criminal prosecutions. That is Article 145(3) of the Federal Constitution."},{t:"p",text:"A charge can end in two very different ways. An acquittal is final. A discharge not amounting to an acquittal is not: the prosecution remains able to recharge on the same facts."},{t:"p",text:"Say that plainly to a client. People hear the word 'discharged' and go home understanding 'finished'."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qbt1",q:"What does 'non-bailable offence' mean?",options:["Bail cannot be granted","Bail requires the Public Prosecutor's consent","Bail is in the court's discretion","Bail is granted only on appeal"],answer:2,why:"The label misleads. Section 388 makes bail discretionary for these offences, subject to the restrictions in it.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code s 388"},{id:"qbt2",q:"Where is an offence classified as bailable or non-bailable?",options:["The Penal Code definition","The First Schedule to the Criminal Procedure Code","The charge sheet","The Courts of Judicature Act 1964"],answer:1,why:"In the First Schedule to the Code, offence by offence. The Penal Code definition does not say.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code, First Schedule"},{id:"qbt3",q:"How long may a person be held after arrest without a Magistrate's authority?",options:["Forty-eight hours","Seven days","Twenty-four hours","Until investigations conclude"],answer:2,why:"Art 5(4) sets twenty-four hours. Anything longer needs a remand order under s 117 of the Code.",source:"Federal Constitution Art 5(4)"},{id:"qbt4",q:"What is the question at the close of the prosecution case?",options:["Whether guilt is proved beyond reasonable doubt","Whether a prima facie case has been made out","Whether the accused has a defence","Whether the charge is properly framed"],answer:1,why:"Section 180 asks whether a prima facie case is made out. If it is, the accused is called to enter his defence.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code s 180"},{id:"qbt5",q:"At the close of the defence, what must the accused's evidence achieve?",options:["Prove innocence on the balance of probabilities","Be believed by the court","Prove innocence beyond reasonable doubt","Raise a reasonable doubt"],answer:3,why:"The prosecution's burden never shifts, save where a statute shifts it. The defence need not be believed.",source:"Standard criminal standard of proof; the prosecution's burden does not shift save where a statute shifts it"},{id:"qbt6",q:"What is the effect of a discharge not amounting to an acquittal?",options:["The prosecution is at an end","The accused may be recharged on the same facts","It bars any further proceedings","It is the same as an acquittal"],answer:1,why:"It is not a final determination, which is exactly why the difference has to be explained to a client.",source:"Criminal Procedure Code; a discharge not amounting to an acquittal is not a final determination"}]}];export{e as default};
